.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESS-UPDATE-SECRET 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ess-update-secret \- rotate a secret, or change what protects it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ess update-secret
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-value\fR|\fB\-v\fR \fITEXT\fR |
\fB\-\-value\-file\fR|\fB\-f\fR \fIFILE\fR |
\fB\-\-value\-stdin\fR]
[\fB\-\-description\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fITEXT\fR]
[\fB\-\-key\fR|\fB\-k\fR \fIERN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Changes a secret that already exists: its value, its description, or
the key it is encrypted under. Only what is named changes, so a
rotation does not have to restate the description and a change of
description does not touch the value.
.SH ROTATING
Setting a value is a rotation. The version moves on, the previous value
is gone \- there is no history to fall back to \- and an
.I ess.secret.rotated
event is published, so anything holding the old value can reload rather
than finding out when it next fails to connect.
.PP
.RS
.nf
openssl rand -base64 24 | euclid-cli ess update-secret \e
    --name db-password --value-stdin
.fi
.RE
.PP
Rotate the thing itself first and euclid second, or the other way
round, depending on which failure you would rather have: euclid ahead
of the database means a window where the stored password does not work
yet, behind it a window where it no longer does.
.SH RE-KEYING
Naming
.B \-\-key
re-encrypts the secret under another EKM key without changing the value
or the version \- how a secret is moved off a key that is being
retired, and why revoking a key does not have to mean losing what was
written under it. The old key is only read, never changed; see
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-revoke-key (1),
which blocks a key from encrypting anything new while leaving it able
to decrypt.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Secret name. Required.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-value " " \fITEXT\fR
The new value. Visible in the process list and the shell history \-
prefer the two below. See
.BR euclid-cli-ess-create-secret (1).
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-value\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Read the new value from a file.
.TP
.B \-\-value\-stdin
Read the new value from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-description " " \fITEXT\fR
What the secret is for. An empty string clears it.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIERN\fR
ERN of another EKM key to encrypt it under from now on.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The secret was changed; its metadata is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing was named to change, no secret of that name exists, the caller
was not granted it, or the key named cannot encrypt.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ess (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-create-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-get-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-revoke-key (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jens Vogt <jens.vogt@opitz-consulting.com>
